**11. Data Quality Assessment**

Not all data is equal. For each layer, we assess quality and define
minimum thresholds:

**11.1 Quality Criteria per Layer**

**Genomic:** Minimum 20% tumor content for WES calls (per SU2C
protocol). Matched normal required for somatic mutation calling. Minimum
30X coverage.

**Transcriptomic (bulk):** Minimum 20M mapped reads. TPM normalization
preferred over FPKM. Batch correction required for cross-dataset
integration.

**Transcriptomic (scRNA-seq):** Minimum 500 genes per cell, 200 cells
per cluster for statistical power. For RNA velocity: minimum 10%
unspliced fraction (below this, velocity estimates are unreliable). 10X
Chromium protocol preferred.

**Drug sensitivity:** GDSC2 preferred over GDSC1 where duplicate exists.
Minimum 5 concentration points per dose-response curve. Cell line
identity verified by STR profiling.

**PK/PD parameters:** FDA-approved drug labels as primary source. Phase
I publications for validation. Population PK models preferred over
single-study estimates.

**Pathway:** STRING confidence score ≥ 0.7 (high confidence) for disease
net edges. Experimental evidence preferred over text-mining.

**11.2 Known Data Gaps**

**Gap 1:** No public scRNA-seq dataset of mCRPC patients receiving
combination therapy (e.g., olaparib + abiraterone) with pre- and
post-treatment biopsies. We must infer combination effects from
single-drug resistance models.

**Gap 2:** PK parameters for drug combinations (drug-drug interactions
affecting PK) are limited. We use independent PK models as first
approximation and flag interaction risks.

**Gap 3:** RNA velocity in clinical biopsies (fresh tissue, not FFPE) is
rare. Most scRNA-seq datasets are from cell lines or fresh-frozen
surgical specimens, not treatment biopsies.

**Gap 4:** Synergy data for prostate cancer-specific combinations is
sparse. NCI-ALMANAC uses NCI-60 panel which has limited prostate
representation (DU145, PC-3 only).

**12. Priority Data Acquisition Sequence**

Based on Phase 1 build roadmap, data must be acquired in this exact
order:

**12.1 Weeks 1--2: Foundation Data**

**Download 1:** GDSC2 drug sensitivity data (IC50 + expression) from
cancerrxgene.org

**Download 2:** TCGA-PRAD RNA-seq from GDC (for KAALCURA
residualization)

**Download 3:** SU2C/PCF 2015 + 2019 from cBioPortal (mCRPC genomic
landscape)

**Download 4:** PK parameters from FDA drug labels (5 drugs: docetaxel,
abiraterone, enzalutamide, olaparib, talazoparib)

**12.2 Weeks 3--6: Validation Data**

**Download 5:** CHAARTED individual patient data from Project Data
Sphere (requires application)

**Download 6:** KEGG + Reactome + STRING pathways for AR signaling, HRR,
EMT, PI3K/AKT

**Download 7:** AlphaFold structures for AR, CYP17A1, PARP1, BRCA2, PTEN

**12.3 Weeks 7--14: RNA Velocity Data**

**Download 8:** scRNA-seq datasets (GSE193337, GSE206962) --- verify
unspliced read availability

**Download 9:** LNCaP enzalutamide resistance scRNA-seq (Deblois et al.
2021) from GEO

**Download 10:** Treatment-resistant PCa scRNA-seq (Sawyers/PNAS 2024)
from GEO

**13. Next Step: ODE + KAALCURA Mathematical Formulation**

With ground truth defined (Document 1) and data sources audited (this
document), the next systematic step is the precise mathematical
specification of our computational engine: the two-population ODE model
equations, KAALCURA axis computation, synergy scoring formulas, and
their coupling. This will be documented in:
INTERCEPTA_Phase1_Mathematical_Specification_v1.0.docx

